WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Steps for Calculating a Performance Baseline

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Hello, Mike Harry back again where we’re looking at baselining

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.100
and determining our opportunity error rate.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:17.500
Now, let's say that per unit we have four opportunities. Again, what is an opportunity?

5
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.500
An opportunity is just that.

6
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:26.333
It is the chance of a defect, it's an opportunity for a defect.

7
00:00:26.333 --> 00:00:31.433
And if there are four such opportunities per unit.

8
00:00:31.433 --> 00:00:36.833
Again, let's go back and see what we mean by that. Back to the idea of banking.

9
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:43.099
Recall in this banking example, people are coming and going. And they come up here.

10
00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:49.666
If the person calls up the wrong account. The person could fail to greet them.

11
00:00:49.666 --> 00:00:55.966
This particular teller right here could fail to smile.

12
00:00:55.966 --> 00:01:01.532
Fail to offer a particular service while the customer is there.

13
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:08.566
An opportunity could be anything. Now, if all of those errors, in this example,

14
00:01:08.566 --> 00:01:14.332
50 such errors are made out of a thousand units and that's our unit error rate.

15
00:01:14.333 --> 00:01:19.933
Given that there’s 4 opportunities, or what we might call defect categories,

16
00:01:19.933 --> 00:01:25.999
then I simply take .05 divided by 4 and that's .0125

17
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.800
opportunities, defects per opportunity, errors per opportunity,

18
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:35.800
consequences per opportunity if you will.

19
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:41.766
Now, step number three is really simple. We take that opportunity error rate

20
00:01:41.766 --> 00:01:48.732
multiply it by a million, and get 12,500 defects per million opportunities.

21
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:53.566
That's the baseline that's kind of the benchmark, if you will.

22
00:01:53.566 --> 00:01:58.266
12,500 defects per million opportunities.

23
00:01:58.266 --> 00:02:04.532
Now, if I know the defects per million opportunities, I can directly compare now.

24
00:02:04.533 --> 00:02:10.933
The defects per million opportunities of this teller.

25
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:17.966
Of this jumper.

26
00:02:17.966 --> 00:02:23.799
The defects per million delivery opportunities.

27
00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:30.833
And the defects per million opportunities for deploying an airbag.

28
00:02:30.833 --> 00:02:34.333
Because it's on a level playing field.

29
00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:39.166
The defect rate has been reduced per million opportunities,

30
00:02:39.166 --> 00:02:42.766
so that everything's on a level playing field, at this point.

31
00:02:42.766 --> 00:02:47.766
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
